Patches against upstream FireSim (tag 1.21.0) required to build and run a
FireSim simulator on the Alveo U55C under Vitis/Vivado 2024.2.

Apply from the root of a FireSim checkout:

    cd <firesim>
    patch -p1 < 0001-rebase-target-cycle-counter.patch
    patch -p1 < 0002-vitis-u55c-build-fixes.patch
    patch -p1 < 0003-vitis-memcheck-and-buffer-lifetime.patch

Apply in order: 0003 touches simulation.{h,cc} and assumes 0001 is already in.

0001-rebase-target-cycle-counter.patch
    Correctness fix. The target-cycle counter lives in FPGA fabric and
    persists while the bitstream stays programmed, but simulation_t reported
    it as an absolute value. Every driver invocation against a resident
    xclbin therefore inherited the previous run's count -- observed as
    13, 26, 39, ... cycles of drift across successive runs of the same test,
    with FMR and Effective Target Frequency corrupted alongside it. Host
    cycles were already rebased in the same function; target cycles now are
    too. Host-side only, no bitstream rebuild needed.

0002-vitis-u55c-build-fixes.patch
    Build-flow fixes. Upstream's Vitis flow targets Vitis 2022.1 and a U250;
    neither assumption holds here. See the patch header for the four
    independent failures and their causes.

0003-vitis-memcheck-and-buffer-lifetime.patch
    Correctness fix plus the diagnostic that settled the FASED-on-HBM
    question. The xrt::bo backing host_mem_0 was a constructor local, so its
    device allocation was freed before the simulation ran. +memcheck
    round-trips a pattern through the LoadMem widget before target execution,
    isolating the target DRAM path from the design under test; on the U55C it
    reports an address-independent constant, unchanged by zeroing DRAM --
    i.e. the HBM memory path never accesses memory, while the MMIO/control
    plane is fully functional. See
    docs/proposals/firesim_vitis_host_mem_offset.md.

All are candidates for upstreaming: only the HOST_MEM_SP default is
U55C-specific, and it is parameterized rather than hardcoded.
